Part B — 🔄 Data Transformation

9. Pandas apply() and map()

apply()

apply() applies a function to Series or DataFrame data.

import pandas as pd

df = pd.DataFrame({
    "Marks": [50, 60, 70]
})

df["Double"] = df["Marks"].apply(
    lambda x: x * 2
)

print(df)


---

map()

map() is mainly used with a Pandas Series for element-wise transformation.

df["Grade"] = df["Marks"].map(
    lambda x: "Pass" if x >= 40 else "Fail"
)

apply() vs map()

apply()	map()

More flexible	Mainly used with Series
Can operate on Series/DataFrame	Element-wise Series mapping
Supports custom functions	Useful for transformation/replacement


⭐ Easy Memory Trick

> map() → Series values
apply() → More flexible function application




---

10. Window Functions

Window functions calculate values over a specified window of data.

Common Pandas window operations:

rolling()

expanding()



---

10.1 rolling()

Calculates values using a fixed-size moving window.

df["Moving_Average"] = (
    df["Marks"].rolling(3).mean()
)

Example:

10, 20, 30 → 20
20, 30, 40 → 30
30, 40, 50 → 40

⭐ Use

Useful for:

Moving averages

Time-series analysis

Trend analysis



---

10.2 expanding()

The window continuously grows.

df["Cumulative_Average"] = (
    df["Marks"].expanding().mean()
)

Example:

10              → 10
10,20           → 15
10,20,30        → 20
10,20,30,40     → 25

Difference

rolling()	expanding()

Fixed window	Growing window
Moving calculation	Cumulative calculation
Example: last 3 values	Example: all values so far



