Part C — 📄 JSON Data

11. What is JSON?

JSON stands for JavaScript Object Notation.

It is a lightweight format used to store and exchange structured data.

Example

{
    "name": "Swarup",
    "age": 20,
    "city": "Jalgaon"
}


---

12. Reading JSON with Pandas

import pandas as pd

df = pd.read_json("students.json")

print(df)


---

13. JSON Record Path Normalization

Nested JSON can be difficult to analyze directly.

json_normalize() converts nested JSON into a flatter tabular structure.

from pandas import json_normalize

data = [
    {
        "name": "Rahul",
        "details": {
            "age": 20,
            "city": "Pune"
        }
    }
]

df = json_normalize(data)

print(df)

Possible output:

name	details.age	details.city

Rahul	20	Pune


⭐ Exam Point

> json_normalize() converts nested JSON into a DataFrame-friendly flat structure.




