Part G — 📈 Data Visualization

18. What is Data Visualization?

Data Visualization is the process of representing data using graphs and charts.

Why Use Visualization?

👀 Understand data quickly

🔍 Find patterns

📈 Identify trends

🚨 Detect outliers

⚖️ Compare variables

📊 Communicate insights


Popular Libraries

import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import seaborn as sns


---

19. Basic Structure of a Plot

A plot may contain:

Figure

Axes

Title

X-axis label

Y-axis label

Ticks

Legend

Plot

Subplots


Example

import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

x = [1, 2, 3, 4]
y = [10, 20, 30, 40]

plt.plot(x, y)

plt.title("Sales Trend")
plt.xlabel("Month")
plt.ylabel("Sales")

plt.show()


---

20. Figure and Axes

fig, ax = plt.subplots()

ax.plot(
    [1, 2, 3],
    [10, 20, 30]
)

ax.set_title("Example")
ax.set_xlabel("X")
ax.set_ylabel("Y")

plt.show()

Difference

Figure → Complete drawing area.

Axes → Area where the actual graph is plotted.


---

21. Legends

A legend identifies different lines or categories.

plt.plot(
    [1, 2, 3],
    [10, 20, 30],
    label="Sales"
)

plt.legend()
plt.show()


---

22. Ticks and Labels

plt.xticks([1, 2, 3])
plt.yticks([10, 20, 30])

plt.xlabel("Month")
plt.ylabel("Sales")


---

23. Subplots

Subplots allow multiple graphs in one figure.

fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2)

axes[0].plot(
    [1, 2, 3],
    [10, 20, 30]
)

axes[1].bar(
    [1, 2, 3],
    [20, 30, 40]
)

plt.show()


---

24. Plot Customization

Marker

plt.plot(
    x, y,
    marker="o"
)

Line Style

plt.plot(
    x, y,
    linestyle="--"
)

Axis Limits

plt.xlim(0, 5)
plt.ylim(0, 50)

Colormap

Colormaps represent numerical values using different shades.


