Part H — 🔎 Univariate Analysis

25. What is Univariate Analysis?

Univariate analysis studies one variable at a time.

Example:

> Analyze the distribution of Salary.



Common plots:

Histogram

KDE

Box Plot

Violin Plot



---

26. Histogram

A histogram displays the distribution and frequency of numerical data.

plt.hist(df["Marks"])

plt.xlabel("Marks")
plt.ylabel("Frequency")

plt.show()

Used For

Distribution

Frequency

Detecting skewness

Understanding spread



---

27. KDE Plot

KDE = Kernel Density Estimate

It shows a smooth estimate of the distribution.

sns.kdeplot(
    data=df,
    x="Marks"
)

plt.show()


---

28. Box Plot

A box plot summarizes:

Minimum

Q1

Median

Q3

Maximum

Potential outliers


sns.boxplot(
    data=df,
    x="Marks"
)

plt.show()


---

29. Violin Plot

A violin plot combines features of:

Box plot

Density plot


sns.violinplot(
    data=df,
    x="Marks"
)

plt.show()


---

30. Bar Plot

A bar plot is mainly used to compare categories.

sns.barplot(
    data=df,
    x="Department",
    y="Salary"
)

plt.show()


---

31. Count Plot

A count plot shows the number of observations in each category.

sns.countplot(
    data=df,
    x="Department"
)

plt.show()


---

Part I — 🔗 Bivariate & Multivariate Analysis

32. Bivariate Analysis

Bivariate analysis studies two variables.

Example:

Study Hours ↔ Marks


---

33. Scatter Plot

A scatter plot shows the relationship between two numerical variables.

sns.scatterplot(
    data=df,
    x="Hours",
    y="Marks"
)

plt.show()

Best For

> Numerical variable ↔ Numerical variable




---

34. Heatmap

A heatmap represents values using different shades and is commonly used for correlation matrices.

sns.heatmap(
    df.corr(numeric_only=True),
    annot=True
)

plt.show()

⭐ Exam Point

> Heatmaps are commonly used to visualize correlation between numerical variables.




---

35. Line Plot

Line plots are useful for trends over time or ordered observations.

sns.lineplot(
    data=df,
    x="Month",
    y="Sales"
)

plt.show()


---

36. KDE Plot with Categories

KDE can compare distributions across groups.

sns.kdeplot(
    data=df,
    x="Marks",
    hue="Gender"
)

plt.show()


---

37. Box Plot with Categories

Useful for comparing numerical distributions between categories.

sns.boxplot(
    data=df,
    x="Department",
    y="Salary"
)

plt.show()


---

38. Histogram with Categories

sns.histplot(
    data=df,
    x="Marks",
    hue="Gender"
)

plt.show()


---

39. Boxen Plot

A boxen plot provides more detailed information about distributions and can be particularly useful for larger datasets.

sns.boxenplot(
    data=df,
    x="Department",
    y="Salary"
)

plt.show()


---

40. Violin Plot with Categories

sns.violinplot(
    data=df,
    x="Department",
    y="Salary"
)

plt.show()


---

41. Count Plot

Used to compare the number of observations in categories.

sns.countplot(
    data=df,
    x="Department"
)

plt.show()


---

42. Stacked Bar Plot

A stacked bar chart displays multiple categories inside each bar.

pd.crosstab(
    df["Department"],
    df["Gender"]
).plot(
    kind="bar",
    stacked=True
)

plt.show()


---

43. Pairplot

A pairplot shows relationships between multiple numerical variables.

sns.pairplot(df)

plt.show()

It generally contains:

Scatter plots between variables

Distribution plots on the diagonal



---

44. Multivariate Analysis

Multivariate analysis studies three or more variables simultaneously.

Example

Age + Salary + Experience

Useful Visualizations

Pairplot

Heatmap

Grouped plots

Facet plots



